<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-laplace-pfd-quadratic" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Inverse Laplace with an Irreducible Quadratic Factor</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TABLE REFERENCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Keep the Laplace transform table handy as a reference for this page.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-laplace-ref-4-2-3">
    <title>Laplace Transform Table</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}</m></cell>
        <cell>Condition</cell>
        <cell>Formula</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>A</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>B</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>C</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>D</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>E</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>H</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\sin(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{b}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>I</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\cos(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>J</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-inverse-laplace-quadratic">
    <title>Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{s^3+4s}\right\}</m></title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Notice that <m>s^3 + 4s = s(s^2+4)</m>, and <m>s^2+4</m> is irreducible since
        <m>s^2 + k</m> with <m>k \gt 0</m> has no real roots.
        This expression does not appear in the table, so we use partial fraction decomposition.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── TASK 1: PFD ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-quadratic-pfd">
      <title>Step 1: Partial Fraction Decomposition</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          From the review page, the correct form for a linear factor and an irreducible quadratic is:
          <me>\frac{1}{s(s^2+4)} = \frac{A}{s} + \frac{Bs+C}{s^2+4}.</me>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Solving for A, B, and C (can skim, reference for homework)</title>
          <p>
            Multiply both sides by <m>s(s^2+4)</m> to clear denominators:
            <me>1 = A(s^2+4) + (Bs+C)s.</me>
            Expand:
            <md>
              <mrow>1 \amp= As^2 + 4A + Bs^2 + Cs</mrow>
              <mrow>\underbrace{0}_{\text{coeff of }s^2} \cdot s^2
                + \underbrace{0}_{\text{coeff of }s} \cdot s
                + \underbrace{1}_{\text{const}}
                \amp= \underbrace{(A+B)}_{\text{coeff of }s^2} s^2
                   + \underbrace{C}_{\text{coeff of }s} s
                   + \underbrace{4A}_{\text{const}}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Matching coefficients:
            <md>
              <mrow>A + B \amp= 0</mrow>
              <mrow>C \amp= 0</mrow>
              <mrow>4A \amp= 1.</mrow>
            </md>
            From the third equation <m>A = \dfrac{1}{4}</m>, then <m>B = -A = -\dfrac{1}{4}</m>,
            and <m>C = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          With <m>A = \dfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>B = -\dfrac{1}{4}</m>, and <m>C = 0</m>,
          the decomposition is:
          <me>\frac{1}{s(s^2+4)} = \frac{\frac{1}{4}}{s} + \frac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK 2: MATCH TO TABLE ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-quadratic-match">
      <title>Step 2: Match Each Term to the Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Looking at the <xref ref="table-laplace-ref-4-2-3">table above</xref>,
          which formulas apply to the two terms
          <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}</m> and <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}</m> matches Formula A, and
              <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m> matches Formula E.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Formula A gives <m>\mathcal{L}\{1\} = \dfrac{1}{s}</m>,
              and Formula E gives <m>\mathcal{L}\{\cos(at)\} = \dfrac{s}{s^2+a^2}</m>.
              The second term has <m>s</m> in the numerator, which matches the cosine formula.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}</m> matches Formula A, and
              <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m> matches Formula D.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula D gives <m>\mathcal{L}\{\sin(at)\} = \dfrac{a}{s^2+a^2}</m>,
              which requires a constant in the numerator. The second term has <m>s</m>
              in the numerator, which matches Formula E (cosine) instead.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}</m> matches Formula B, and
              <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m> matches Formula E.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula B gives <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>,
              which has a shifted denominator <m>s-a</m>. Here the denominator is just <m>s</m>,
              which matches Formula A with <m>f(t) = 1</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Both terms match Formula B.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula B applies to terms of the form <m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>.
              Neither term here has that form.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK 3: COMPUTE ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-quadratic-compute">
      <title>Step 3: Apply the Inverse Transform</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the decomposition from Step 1 and the formulas identified in Step 2,
          compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{s^3+4s}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The decomposition is <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s} + \dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m>.
          <ul>
            <li><p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}</m> matches <xref ref="formula-A" text="custom">Formula A</xref>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+4}</m> matches <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref>
              with <m>a = 2</m>. You may need to rewrite slightly to match the form <m>\dfrac{s}{s^2+a^2}</m>.
            </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <title>Work</title>
        <p>
          Applying linearity and the inverse transform to each piece:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s(s^2+4)}\right\}
              \amp= \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
                     \underbrace{\frac{\frac{1}{4}}{s}}_{\text{Formula A}}
                   \right\}
                   + \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
                     \underbrace{\frac{-\frac{1}{4}s}{s^2+2^2}}_{\text{Formula E},\, a=2}
                   \right\}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{4} \cdot 1 - \frac{1}{4}\cos(2t)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{4}\cos(2t).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          When the denominator contains an irreducible quadratic <m>s^2+k</m>,
          the partial fraction form uses a linear numerator <m>Bs+C</m> for that factor.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          After decomposing, match each term to the table. A constant numerator over <m>s^2+a^2</m>
          matches <xref ref="formula-D" text="custom">Formula D</xref> (sine),
          and an <m>s</m> numerator over <m>s^2+a^2</m> matches
          <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref> (cosine).
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <m>s^2+k</m> is always irreducible when <m>k \gt 0</m>, so it always gets
          a linear numerator in the partial fraction form.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

